<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schengen Area</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Schengen zone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schengen Area is a zone of European countries that have abolished passport and border controls at their common borders. It grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-agreement">Schengen Agreement</a>, signed in 1985 at Schengen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, and the 1990 convention implementing it, which took effect in 1995; it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> law in 1999. It covers most EU member states and the non-members <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schengen-area.jpg" alt="Schengen Area" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Schengen_Area.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> most countries of the world, including those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, had lax border policies, which made such educational trips as the Grand Tour possible among the wealthy. The first move towards abolishing border controls between members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> came on 14 June 1985, when five of the then ten EEC members signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-agreement">Schengen Agreement</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a> countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>. They did so separately from the European Communities, because consensus could not be reached among all member states.</p><p>The agreement, on the gradual abolition of checks at the signatories' common borders, was supplemented by the 1990 Schengen Convention. The area originally had its legal basis outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, in these two international agreements made by a subset of its members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> incorporated the Schengen rules, known as the Schengen acquis, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> law, and the change took effect on 1 May 1999. When the treaty was negotiated, only Ireland and the United Kingdom had not signed the agreement. The United Kingdom chose not to join, and Ireland, which wished to keep its Common Travel Area with the United Kingdom, also declined; both negotiated an opt-out. In 1999 the United Kingdom asked to take part in provisions on police and judicial cooperation, which the Council approved on 29 May 2000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> adopted the Schengen rules on an intergovernmental basis under a specific opt-out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, members of the Nordic Passport Union, became associated with the Schengen activities of the Union, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> was allowed to take part in the same manner in 2008 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> in 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A state that wishes to join is assessed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> on border control legislation, infrastructure and organisation, personal data protection, visas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and police cooperation. It must take responsibility for controlling the Union's external borders, apply common rules for land, sea and air frontiers, issue uniform Schengen visas, cooperate with the law enforcement agencies of other members and connect to the Schengen Information System. Admission then requires a unanimous decision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of the European Union</a> after consulting the European Parliament.</p><p>Some territories have special arrangements: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> are fully inside the area with specific border control arrangements, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a> and Svalbard are excluded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> is administered as part of the area through its open border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under a 1963 convention. Andorra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> have open or semi-open borders with their neighbours without being formal members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schengen Area” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lithuania joined NATO and the European Union in 2004, the <b>Schengen Area</b> in 2007 and the eurozone in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Schengen Area</b> now covers most of the European Union and several other states.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The country fully joined the <b>Schengen area</b> in 2025 and adopted the euro in 2026.</blockquote><a href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the eurozone and the <b>Schengen Area</b> in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/state/croatia">Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>The country joined the OECD in 1995, NATO in 1999, the European Union in 2004 and the <b>Schengen Area</b> in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/state/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Poland joined NATO in 1999 and, after a referendum in June 2003, the European Union on 1 May 2004, and entered the <b>Schengen Area</b> in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/state/poland">Poland</a></li></ul></section>
